Given, \(A_{OL}=100,\) \(f_L=100\,\text{Hz},\) \(f_H=100\,\text{kHz},\) and \(\beta=0.99\) The desensitivity factor is: \(D=1+A_{OL}\beta
=1+100\times0.99
=100\) The lower cut-off frequency with feedback is: \(f_{Lf}=\dfrac{f_L}{D}
=\dfrac{100}{100}
=1\,\text{Hz}\) The upper cut-off frequency with feedback is: \(f_{Hf}=f_HD
=100\times10^3\times100
=10^7\,\text{Hz}
=10\,\text{MHz}\) \[\boxed{1\,\text{Hz},\;10\,\text{MHz}}\]

A narrowband Gaussian noise signal can be represented as \[n(t)
=
a(t)\cos(2\pi f_ct)
-
b(t)\sin(2\pi f_ct)\]where \(a(t)\) and \(b(t)\) are independent zero mean Gaussian random variables having equal variance. The envelope of the noise signal is \[R=\sqrt{a^2(t)+b^2(t)}\]Since the envelope is the magnitude of two independent Gaussian components, its probability density function follows the Rayleigh distribution. <strong>Rayleigh Probability Density Function</strong> \[f_R(r)
=
\frac{r}{\sigma^2}
e^{-r^2/(2\sigma^2)},
\qquad r\ge0\] \[\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
(D)\;&amp; \text{For a narrow band noise with Gaussian quadrature and inphase components, the probability} \\
&amp; \text{ density function of its envelope will be Rayleigh.} \\
\end{aligned}
}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Rayleigh Envelope Distribution:</strong> The in-phase and quadrature components are Gaussian random variables. When their vector magnitude is taken, \[R=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\] the resulting envelope follows a Rayleigh distribution.</li></ul></div>
